<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palatine Hill</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Palatium, Palatino</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palatine Hill is the centremost of the seven hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and one of the oldest parts of the city. Excavations show that people lived there from the 10th century BCE, and in Roman mythology it held the Lupercal cave where the she-wolf kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> and Remus alive, and was where Romulus founded the city. Before imperial times the hill was mostly occupied by the houses of the rich; from Augustus onward emperors built their palaces there, and its name is the origin of the word palace. The site is now largely an open-air museum, with the Palatine Museum holding finds from its excavations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palatine-hill.jpg" alt="Palatine Hill" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lil Herodotus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palatine_Hill_from_across_the_Circus_Maximus_April_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The hill originally had two summits separated by a depression: the higher was called Palatium and the lower Germalus, or Cermalus. Livy derived the name from Arcadian settlers from Pallantium, named after Pallas, son of Lycaon; a more likely derivation is from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> noun palātum, 'palate', which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennius">Ennius</a> uses once for 'heaven' and which may connect with the Etruscan word for sky, falad. The name is the origin of the word palace in many languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations in 1907 and again in 1948 uncovered a collection of huts believed to have been used for funerary purposes between the 9th and 7th centuries BCE. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> lived on the Palatine after the Sabines and Albans came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the hill was the site of the festival of the Lupercalia. From 27 BCE Augustus built his palace there, with a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, and the hill gradually became the exclusive domain of emperors; ruins of the palaces of Augustus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> can still be seen. The Great Fire of Rome in 64 CE destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s Domus Transitoria, which he replaced by 69 CE with the larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domus-aurea">Domus Aurea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>From the 16th century the Farnese family owned the hill and laid out the Farnese Gardens over the remains of the Domus Tiberiana. The Villa Mattei became a convent at the end of the 19th century and was partly demolished from 1928 to allow excavation, and the Palatine Museum now stands in the surviving part. In 2006 archaeologists announced the Palatine House, believed to be the birthplace of Augustus. In 2007 a cavity beneath the House of Livia was claimed to be the Lupercal, though many deny this and take it for a nymphaeum or underground dining room of Neronian date.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palatine Hill” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Colosseum stands on the floor of a low valley between the Caelian, Esquiline and <b>Palatine Hills</b>, through which a canalised stream ran.</blockquote><a href="/work/colosseum">Colosseum · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Standing between the Colosseum and the <b>Palatine Hill</b>, it is the largest Roman triumphal arch.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-25">25 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
